"""Reference solution for ``aten::softplus_backward``.

The declared entrypoint ``run`` mirrors the operator ABI that both frozen
callsites actually use:

    run(grad_output, self, beta, threshold) -> grad_input

* accuracy (prepared/tests/test_softplus.py, line 117-119):
  ``flag_gems.softplus_backward(grad, inp, beta=beta, threshold=threshold)``
* benchmark (prepared/benchmark/test_softplus_backward.py, builds
  ``(grad_output, self, 1.5, 20.0)`` and times the injected callable)

The frozen Definition (definitions/softplus_backward.json) declares
``grad_output``/``self`` as Tensors, ``beta``/``threshold`` as float Scalars,
one output ``grad_input``, ``mutates: []`` and ``returns_alias_of: {}``. The
native aten operator already satisfies that contract: it is out-of-place,
returns a fresh dense tensor with the shape/dtype/device of ``self``, and
leaves both inputs untouched. So the wrap returns the aten result directly
(no ``clone``/``contiguous`` re-encoding, no in-place reuse of an input
buffer) and does not touch either operand.

This module imports only ``torch`` and calls the native aten kernel, never the
FlagGems candidate that is being replaced, so injecting it as the candidate
cannot recurse into itself and it is not its own oracle.
"""

import torch


def run(grad_output, self, beta, threshold):
    return torch.ops.aten.softplus_backward(
        grad_output,
        self,
        beta=beta,
        threshold=threshold,
    )
